faq:
  - q: "How do I network for a job without it feeling fake?"
    a: "Network with people you have a genuine connection to or a genuine reason to reach out. Most networking feels fake because it is fake — generic messages with no specific reason for contact. The antidote: reach out only when you have something specific to say (a post you read, a shared connection, a specific question about their role or company), and frame it as information exchange, not a favor ask. Genuine curiosity converts; manufactured networking does not."
  - q: "Who should I contact when networking for a job search?"
    a: "In priority order: (1) Former managers — the highest-converting contact in any job search. They know your work and have credibility to refer you. (2) Former colleagues who are now at target companies — they can refer you directly into open roles. (3) Current contacts in your field at peer companies — for market intelligence and introduction chains. (4) Recruiters who specialize in your function — both in-house at target companies and agency recruiters who place roles like yours. (5) Alumni from your university at target companies — shared alumni status converts cold outreach to warm."
  - q: "What should I say when networking for a job?"
    a: "Three components: who you are (brief), what you are looking for (specific), and the ask (small and easy). Example: 'Hi [Name], I'm a senior data engineer who spent the last 4 years building real-time pipelines at [Company] — currently exploring new opportunities. I noticed you are at [Company] on the data platform team. Would you be open to 20 minutes to share your perspective on the team and the type of problems you are working on?' Small ask, specific context, no pressure."
  - q: "Is LinkedIn the best platform for job search networking?"
    a: "LinkedIn is the most efficient platform for professional networking — it gives you access to direct contact information and context (role, company, tenure) for every professional contact. It is not the only platform — warm email introductions from mutual contacts convert better than cold LinkedIn messages. Use LinkedIn to find who to contact; use warm introductions to reach them whenever possible."
  - q: "How often should I follow up after a networking outreach?"
    a: "Once — after 5–7 business days with no response. A single professional follow-up is appropriate. More than two follow-ups on a cold outreach becomes spam. If there is still no response after the follow-up, move on. The contact is either not available, not interested, or missed the message. Persistence in networking is about volume of new contacts, not repeated pushes on unresponsive ones."
